# Security Policy

Thank you for helping keep Codexeron and our community safe.

We take security seriously and appreciate responsible disclosure of security vulnerabilities.

---

# Supported Versions

The following versions currently receive security updates.

| Version | Supported |
|----------|-----------|
| 1.x.x    | ✅ Yes |
| < 1.0    | ❌ No |

---

# Reporting a Vulnerability

If you discover a security vulnerability, please do not create a public GitHub Issue.

Instead, report it privately.

Contact:

security@codexeron.com

Please include:

- Detailed description
- Steps to reproduce
- Proof of Concept (if available)
- Screenshots
- Expected behavior
- Impact assessment

---

# Response Timeline

We aim to respond according to the following timeline.

Initial Response

Within 48 hours

Investigation

Within 7 business days

Fix Development

Depends on severity.

Security patches are prioritized.

---

# Responsible Disclosure

Please allow us reasonable time to investigate and fix reported vulnerabilities before public disclosure.

We appreciate researchers who follow responsible disclosure practices.

---

# Scope

Examples include:

- Authentication bypass
- Authorization issues
- SQL Injection
- XSS
- CSRF
- Remote Code Execution
- File Upload vulnerabilities
- Privilege Escalation
- Sensitive Data Exposure
- Business Logic flaws

---

# Out of Scope

The following generally do not qualify.

- Missing HTTP headers
- Clickjacking without exploit
- Self-XSS
- Spam reports
- Social engineering
- Physical attacks
- Outdated browser issues

---

# Security Best Practices

Researchers should:

- Avoid accessing unnecessary user data
- Do not interrupt service
- Do not modify customer data
- Avoid automated destructive scanning
- Report findings privately

---

# Future Security Program

Future plans include:

- Security.txt
- PGP encrypted reporting
- Bug Bounty Program
- Security Hall of Fame
- Coordinated Vulnerability Disclosure (CVD)

---

# Thank You

We sincerely appreciate everyone who helps improve the security of Codexeron.
